course: everyday
lesson: 337
title: Excuses and the lies that grow
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: An excuse grows like a snowball. Liam in Toronto said he was at Tom's house when he was really at the park, because he wanted to stay out longer. It worked once, then twice, and he began to think it always would. Then Tom's mum met his mum at the shop. He was kept in after school for a week.
explain: Getting away with a lie three times does not make it safe; it only makes the lie bigger when it comes out, and by then the grown-ups wonder about all the other times too.
explain: Liam's piano excuse went the same way. He said he had practised all week; his teacher could hear the truth in the first two bars. The lesson was wasted, and he had to own up and make a practice chart for the fridge.
explain: Some lies hurt the liar most. Liam said a bruise came from falling, when a boy had pushed him, because he did not want to be a tell-tale. The boy did it again. When he finally told his teacher, she sorted it out in a day. Telling a grown-up when someone hurts you is not telling tales.
explain: Other children in Liam's class learned the same. A girl hid her peas under her plate and said she had eaten them; when the plate was lifted, the peas rolled out. A boy said he was too tired to visit his great-aunt, who had been looking forward to it; he put it right by making her a card and going with Mum the next Sunday.
explain: The fix for an excuse is always the same: tell the real reason, take the consequence, and next time ask properly or say what you are afraid of.
fact: Grown-ups need to know where you really are so they can keep you safe.
fact: A lie that works a few times usually gets found out in the end, often by chance.
fact: Getting away with something does not make it right or safe.
fact: Practice shows in what you can do, not in what you say you did.
fact: Age and height rules on rides are there for safety, not to spoil fun: a smaller body can slip out under the safety bar, and ride staff often measure you anyway.
fact: Telling a trusted grown-up when someone hurts you is not telling tales (being a «grass» or a «snitch»); it is how bullying is stopped.
fact: Hiding a letter from school does not hide the problem; it only hides you from help.
fact: Skipping hand-washing after the toilet can spread tummy bugs to you and your family.
fact: Making an excuse to skip a visit can hurt an old relative who was looking forward to it.
fact: When you are nervous about something, saying so to a parent usually helps more than an excuse.
exercise: Why did Liam say he was at Tom's when he was at the park? | He wanted to stay out longer without asking | He thought the park was part of Tom's garden | He forgot where he was | Tom asked him to say it
exercise: The Tom's-house lie had worked twice before. What does that show? | Nothing about safety: it can still come out any day | That lying gets easier and safer each time you do it | That it will always work | That his mum doesn't mind
exercise: How was Liam's park lie found out? | Tom's mum met his mum at the shop | The park keeper rang his school about it | His shoes were muddy from Tom's garden | His little brother saw him on the TV news
exercise: Why do parents need to know where a child really is? | So they can keep him safe and find him if something goes wrong | So they can choose all of his friends for him and stop the ones they dislike | So they can tell the neighbours | Because it is a law about parks
exercise: A girl hides her peas under the plate and says she ate them. What happens when the plate is lifted? | The peas roll out and the lie is plain | The peas have disappeared into the plate | Nobody looks under plates | The dinner lady eats them
exercise: How can a piano teacher tell a pupil didn't practise? | She hears it in how he plays | She checks the piano for dust | His fingers are a different colour | She phones his neighbours to ask
exercise: A boy says he is ten to go on a ride for over-tens, but he is nine and too short. Why is the rule there? | To keep riders safe, because smaller bodies can slip out | To sell more tickets to older children, who pay more | To make nine-year-olds sad | Because rides are boring for little ones
exercise: Why did a boy say a bruise came from falling when a boy had pushed him? | He did not want to be called a tell-tale | He thought falling sounded braver than being pushed | He forgot who pushed him | The bruise really came from a fall
exercise: What happened while the bruise stayed a secret? | The boy pushed him again for weeks | The bully said sorry all by himself | The bruise made the bully stop at once | Nothing, because secrets keep you safe
exercise: Telling a teacher that someone hurt you is... | Asking for help, not telling tales | Telling tales and getting him in trouble | Only right if you are bleeding badly | Something you should never, ever do
exercise: A girl says the school trip letter never came home, because she is nervous about the trip. What would have helped more? | Telling Mum she was nervous, so Mum could help | Throwing away the next letter too, so it all stays hidden | Asking a friend to hide it | Pretending to be ill on the day
exercise: A boy says he is tired to skip visiting his great-aunt. Who is hurt most? | His great-aunt, who was waiting for him | Nobody at all, since he really was tired | Only his mum's car | The neighbours of the great-aunt
exercise: How did that boy put the visit right? | He made her a card and went with Mum the next Sunday | He promised himself to visit her once he was grown up, in ten years | He sent her a text saying sorry | He told his mum to say he was ill again
exercise: A boy says he washed his hands after the toilet but didn't, then gets a tummy bug and gives it to his brother. What does this show? | Some lies can make people ill | Hand-washing is only for doctors | Tummy bugs come from telling lies | His brother was always going to be ill
exercise: Why does a lie usually grow bigger the longer it lasts? | You need more lies to cover the first one | Grown-ups stop caring about it after a week | Lies are always forgotten | The truth becomes false over time
exercise: What is the wise choice when you want something a grown-up might refuse, like staying out longer? | Ask honestly and accept the answer | Do it and say you were somewhere else | Ask a friend to lie for you | Do it and say nothing
test: «been telling mum im at Tom's for weeks, never caught, so it's fine innit». What is right? | Not getting caught doesn't make it fine, and she needs to know where he is | Yes, if it worked for weeks it's fine, she's never been worried | Fine until he's ten | Fine if Tom agrees
test: Mum's text: «Tom's mum says you weren't there. where were you. home NOW». What is the best thing to do? | Go home at once and tell the truth about the park | Stay out longer, she'll calm down after an hour | Say Tom's mum is confused | Switch the phone off
test: Piano teacher, dry: «All week, was it? Play me bar three.» What will most likely happen? | She'll hear he hasn't practised, so owning up is best | She'll believe him if he plays it really fast and loud | She'll give him a star anyway | She can't tell the difference
test: Big sister: «just say ur 10, they never check». The boy is nine and small. What is wrong with that? | The rule is for safety, and they measure you anyway | Nothing, it's a harmless fib that hurts nobody | Rides never have rules | Nine-year-olds are always tall enough
test: Kid at break: «dont be a grass, say u fell». A boy keeps pushing him. What should he do? | Tell his teacher what really happens | Say he fell, to keep the peace with everyone | Push the boy back harder | Stay inside every playtime
test: Dinner lady, sharp: «Them peas didn't eat themselves, love». What should the girl do? | Say sorry and try a bite of each | Say the peas fell on the floor | Say she's allergic to peas | Say a friend took them
test: The trip letter turns up on the class website. Mum, hurt: «why'd you hide it?» What answer helps most? | The true one: «I was scared about the trip» | «I didn't want you to have to pay for it» | «It never came, honest» | «The website is wrong»
test: Gran: «Was he really too tired to come? I'd made scones.» What would make it right? | He says sorry and visits her next week | He says he was too tired again | He sends someone else instead | He forgets about it
test: Which is TRUE about a lie that has worked three times? | The next time it may be found out, and all three come out together | It is now completely safe, because three times shows that nobody ever checks | It has become true | It only matters if you get caught the first time
test: «u washed ur hands?» «yeah» (he didn't). Why is this lie worse than it sounds? | Germs can spread and make the family ill | Soap is expensive | Hands don't carry germs | Only grown-ups catch tummy bugs
test: A boy keeps saying he's tired to miss things. What does his mum begin to think? | That his excuses can't be trusted, even when he is tired | That he needs more sleep, nothing else, and an early bedtime | That he is very honest | That tiredness is catching
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a deception which has repeatedly escaped discovery. | Prior success affords no assurance that the deception will continue to escape discovery | Repeated success renders the deception permissible, since no harm has thereby been occasioned | A deception undiscovered on three occasions becomes truthful | Prior success guarantees immunity from any consequence
exam: Identify the principal reason for which parents require accurate knowledge of the whereabouts of a child. | It enables them to ensure his safety and to locate him in an emergency | It enables them to regulate his friendships entirely and to forbid any visits | It is required solely for the purpose of punishment | It is of no practical significance
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reporting to a teacher of physical harm inflicted by another pupil? | It constitutes a proper request for assistance and not the telling of tales | It constitutes disloyalty and ought to be avoided | It is appropriate solely where an injury requires hospital treatment | It is invariably of no assistance to the pupil harmed
exam: Ascertain the purpose of minimum age and height requirements applicable to amusement rides. | They serve to protect the safety of riders | They serve solely to diminish the enjoyment of younger children | They serve exclusively to increase the revenue of the operator | They serve no purpose whatsoever
